WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.516 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our school wants a new lunchtime club.

00:00:02.516 --> 00:00:07.255 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Would asking only your football team tell us what all Year 5
pupils want?

00:00:07.255 --> 00:00:11.142 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Picture a tray holding art pencils, a football and a drum.

00:00:11.142 --> 00:00:13.710 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each object suggests a different club.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:19.730 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Your own favourite is a useful starting guess, but a school
decision needs more than one person’s guess.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:24.092 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We need a question and evidence from the people the decision
will affect.

00:00:24.092 --> 00:00:28.146 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to plan a fair way to collect useful
data.

00:00:28.146 --> 00:00:31.589 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our purpose is to help choose a lunchtime club fairly.

00:00:31.589 --> 00:00:38.862 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
By the end, you should be able to explain both what
information you need and why your collection plan will give
each class a voice.

00:00:38.862 --> 00:00:42.260 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep that decision in mind as we sort the information.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.369 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can tell names, ordered groups and counts apart.

00:00:45.369 --> 00:00:48.618 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can pose a question that allows different answers.

00:00:48.618 --> 00:00:52.532 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can choose who to ask and how to record one response each.

00:00:52.532 --> 00:00:57.164 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Remember sorting objects into groups and counting how many
were in each group.

00:00:57.164 --> 00:00:59.612 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will use those familiar skills again.

00:00:59.612 --> 00:01:05.926 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now we also need to notice whether the groups have an order,
and whether a number is a count or just a label.

00:01:05.926 --> 00:01:08.462 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Data are pieces of information.

00:01:08.462 --> 00:01:13.820 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Nominal categories are names with no natural order, such as
art, sport and music.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.328 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ordinal categories have an order, such as low, medium and
high interest.

00:01:18.328 --> 00:01:23.493 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Discrete numerical data are counts, such as the number of
library books borrowed.

00:01:23.493 --> 00:01:27.524 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A statistical question expects different answers across a
group.

00:01:27.524 --> 00:01:30.417 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine handing out three different cards.

00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:33.729 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The first card names a club: art, sport or music.

00:01:33.729 --> 00:01:37.917 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We can arrange those names in any order without changing
their meaning.

00:01:37.917 --> 00:01:41.777 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The second card records low, medium or high interest.

00:01:41.777 --> 00:01:43.280 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That order matters.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.070 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The third card asks for a count of visits.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.830 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Zero visits is a possible answer too.

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:54.249 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which club would each Year 5 pupil choose: art, sport, music
or another club?

00:01:54.249 --> 00:01:57.438 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before you decide, think about the group in the question.

00:01:57.438 --> 00:02:00.664 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Is it one person, or all Year 5 pupils?

00:02:00.664 --> 00:02:03.689 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Quietly choose the information you would collect.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:06.815 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep your reason in mind as you look at the choices.

00:02:06.815 --> 00:02:10.508 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which responses could be different from person to person?

00:02:15.508 --> 00:02:18.776 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This asks about a group and expects variation.

00:02:18.776 --> 00:02:23.098 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask every Year 5 class using the same options, once per
pupil.

00:02:23.098 --> 00:02:27.308 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Include another option so pupils are not forced into a poor
match.

00:02:27.308 --> 00:02:33.518 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Notice that a question about the colour of one particular
ball can be answered by looking at that ball once.

00:02:33.518 --> 00:02:35.598 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our club question is different.

00:02:35.598 --> 00:02:38.304 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Different pupils may choose different clubs.

00:02:38.304 --> 00:02:44.358 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That variation is useful, because it helps us understand the
group instead of assuming everyone agrees.

00:02:44.358 --> 00:02:48.927 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We record club choice, interest level and number of club
visits last term.

00:02:48.927 --> 00:02:50.907 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which can be put in order?

00:02:50.907 --> 00:02:53.402 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look at the three recording columns.

00:02:53.402 --> 00:02:59.079 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Try matching one imaginary pupil’s answers to them: music,
high interest, and two visits.

00:02:59.079 --> 00:03:01.829 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Say which answer belongs in each column.

00:03:01.829 --> 00:03:06.237 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think about what the answer means before deciding its data
type.

00:03:06.237 --> 00:03:10.144 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What would change if another pupil had not visited at all?

00:03:15.144 --> 00:03:16.817 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Club choice is nominal.

00:03:16.817 --> 00:03:20.086 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Interest level is ordinal: low, medium, high.

00:03:20.086 --> 00:03:23.700 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Visits are discrete numbers: 0, 1, 2 and so on.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.962 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A pupil number is a label, not a count of something.

00:03:26.962 --> 00:03:30.356 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A count of two visits tells us how many visits happened.

00:03:30.356 --> 00:03:34.858 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
High interest tells us an ordered category, rather than an
exact amount.

00:03:34.858 --> 00:03:38.826 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Music names a choice, without saying it is above or below
art.

00:03:38.826 --> 00:03:46.067 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We can count responses in any of these groups, but counting
responses does not turn club names into numerical
measurements.

00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:51.730 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
“Numbers always mean numerical data.” A sports shirt number
is a name label.

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:55.125 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask what the value describes, not just how it looks.

00:03:55.125 --> 00:03:58.019 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine a football shirt with the number ten.

00:03:58.019 --> 00:04:02.268 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Adding the shirt numbers would not tell us how many pupils
play football.

00:04:02.268 --> 00:04:04.753 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Those numbers identify shirts.

00:04:04.753 --> 00:04:08.185 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A count of ten pupils answers a different question.

00:04:08.185 --> 00:04:11.825 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The meaning of a number is more important than its
appearance.

00:04:11.825 --> 00:04:14.717 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask yourself, what exactly are we counting?

00:04:14.717 --> 00:04:18.771 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Pause the player and try the investigation in the written
lesson.

00:04:18.771 --> 00:04:23.374 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Make a prediction, collect or change the data, then explain
what you notice.

00:04:23.374 --> 00:04:25.434 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now plan a collection method.

00:04:25.434 --> 00:04:29.264 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask the same neutral question in every Year 5 class.

00:04:29.264 --> 00:04:36.164 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Record one response per pupil, include another-club option,
and decide what to do about pupils who are absent.

00:04:36.164 --> 00:04:39.052 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You do not need names on the final graph.

00:04:39.052 --> 00:04:44.639 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A checked class list can prevent accidental double counting
without making names public.

00:04:44.639 --> 00:04:47.047 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Write a question for a school decision.

00:04:47.047 --> 00:04:52.800 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Name the group you will ask, the variable you will collect
and how you will avoid counting anyone twice.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.058 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check your plan with these questions.

00:04:55.058 --> 00:04:58.106 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Could the wording push pupils towards one answer?

00:04:58.106 --> 00:04:59.913 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Could a whole class be missing?

00:04:59.913 --> 00:05:02.429 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Could the same pupil be counted twice?

00:05:02.429 --> 00:05:05.473 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain one change that would make the plan fairer.

00:05:05.473 --> 00:05:10.011 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A useful investigation begins before the first response is
collected.

00:05:10.011 --> 00:05:15.161 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our evidence can guide a decision when the question, group
and recording method fit together.

00:05:15.161 --> 00:05:17.385 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think about your own school question.

00:05:17.385 --> 00:05:21.211 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What different answers might you receive, and how would you
record them?

00:05:21.211 --> 00:05:25.965 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Share your plan with someone and explain how it gives the
intended group a fair voice.
